  "id": "split-stance-squat",
  "name": "Stationary Split Squat",
  "definition": "A stationary unilateral-emphasis compound exercise performed from a fixed sagittal split stance. Both feet retain their reviewed floor contacts while the athlete lowers until the lead thigh is parallel to the floor, then returns to split standing through lead-side hip extension, knee extension, and ankle plantarflexion. The initial roster owns only an erect-torso barbell split squat at a stance length equal to the athlete's leg length. It does not treat a forward, reverse, or walking lunge's step, impact, deceleration, and changing support phases as variant axes. The family ID names the stationary geometry; the fixed lunge pattern is Vivobody's broader app-level classification for asymmetric split-stance lower-body compounds. Rear-foot-elevated, front-foot-elevated, supported, Smith, dumbbell, kettlebell, bodyweight, deficit, short-stance, long-stance, dynamic-lunge, and walking-lunge branches remain outside this first contract.",
